notes[11]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-26T14:58:06.980Z","harness:claude-code","Root cause, from a tsx trace of the structured pass: (1) compact JSON: the quoted key 'password' inside the title string is judged by findStructuredSpellingMatches, whose value judge reads the concatenating PASSWORD_VALUE grammar without the enclosing-string close that GH-1775 added to flatMatches, so '${BUILD_PASSWORD}' runs on through the JSON string's closing quote into \",\"updated\":\"2026-11-25T00 (pretty JSON hides it because a quoted piece cannot cross the newline). (2) seven backslashes: the single-quoted raw (TOML/YAML) reading reused the escape-aware scanQuoted span, in which the seventh backslash escapes the final quote, so the literal read as unterminated and the quote counted as an eighth data character. Fix: the structured judge ends a text-read value at the enclosing string's close exactly as flatMatches does (cut, and recorded as the word end), through a lexer cursor with checkpoints every 1024 code units for keys nested in already-walked containers; the raw single-quoted reading ends at its own first quote. Differential over an 18,816-input corpus (8 keys x 26 values x quote styles x 7 serializations) against the published 2026.11.14 and 2026.11.25 bundles: 280 verdicts change, every one a finding to none, none on a literal-bearing value; 240 return to the 2026.11.14 verdict and the other 40 are bare $NAME and %NAME% references in quoted-key text, now placeholders as GH-1775 states for every serialization. '${BUILD_PASSWORD}(note)' was clean on both published versions (the parenthesis ends the word); attached literals such as '${BUILD_PASSWORD}<literal>' stay reported."
  "2026-11-26T15:59:07.424Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 on PR #1789 (gpt-6.2-sol xhigh), both P1 findings confirmed and fixed. (1) example foo\"[\" --'password'=${EMPTY}\",\"\"<literal>\": the lexer declines the quote after foo (a quote after a word character opens no string), so the quote after [ opened a phantom container string whose close cut the literal; main still reported it through the uncut structured reading, while its flat twin (--password=...) was already missed since GH-1775. Fix: the lexer records a declined quote per kind until the line ends, and enclosingStringClose trusts no string of that kind opened later on the line (trustsOpenString); both spellings are reported again. (2) the Python dict fixture in secret-guard-string-boundaries.spec.ts (a password of two unrecognized escapes, an escaped apostrophe and four characters): Python keeps unrecognized escapes (nine characters), the decoded reading drops them (seven) and the first-quote raw reading stopped at the escaped quote; main reported it through the escape-aware raw span. Fix: that backslash-kept span is read again whenever the escape-aware scan closes the literal, alongside the first-quote reading; it is skipped only for a span left open by an escaped last quote, which is what produced the seven-backslash false positive. P3 docs: the first-quote reading is described as TOML's and a scanner approximation for YAML. Differential rerun: 276 changes versus 2026.11.25, all finding to none, none literal-bearing, no new findings; the four that reverted to the 2026.11.25 verdict are a seven-backslash value inside a malformed single-quoted repr, where the escape-aware scan closes the span."
  "2026-11-26T16:52:10.174Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 on PR #1789: both secret-guard findings confirmed against main and fixed. (1) A shell option whose single-quoted piece ends in an escaped-looking backslash and has characters joined after it (the round-2 printf fixture in secret-guard-string-boundaries.spec.ts) was reported on main and missed here: the first-quote reading could not see the joined characters, and the backslash-kept span is skipped when left open. The value judge now also applies the text reading to single-quoted literals with backslashes; it is the shell-word and TOML reading (first closing quote, joined pieces kept), and it replaces the separate first-quote reading. (2) A Python raw-string prefix before a JSON-like line (r-quoted text, then a dict member holding a bare reference) was newly reported: one declined quote distrusted every later string on the line. Now only the next string of that kind after an odd run of declined quotes is suspect, and a Python string prefix (r, b, u, f and their two-letter pairs, any case) opens its string, with backslash escapes as Python reads them. lexStep's quote opening moved into openOrDecline to stay within the complexity budget. Differential: 428 changes versus 2026.11.25, none on a literal-bearing value; 152 new findings, all either a quoted literal password inside JSON text that both published versions missed, or backslash-only values in malformed single-quoted reprs where 2026.11.25 already reports the backslash-free spellings."
  "2026-11-26T17:05:59.167Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 3 on PR #1789: two synthetic shell lines desynchronized the narrowed round-2 rule (a second quoted shell argument after a declined quote re-opened a trusted phantom string; a Python-prefixed single quote forced Python escapes where the shell closes the string), and both lost findings main reported. The lexer now follows one principle instead of per-case narrowing: a cut only removes findings, so it is made only where the string state is unambiguous; a line that declined a quote of a kind reads as shell, and no later string of that kind on the line is trusted. Python prefixes still open their string, with the escapes an unprefixed one has. Both lines are in secret-guard-string-boundaries.spec.ts; the round-2 Python-prefix case stays clean; the 18,816-input differential is unchanged from round 2."
  "2026-11-26T17:16:19.436Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 on PR #1789: (1) fixed: an ANSI-C dollar-quoted string escapes its quote like a double-quoted one, so its real closing quote no longer opens a phantom container string that cut a shell literal (spec case added). (2) deferred to pm-rzojed: the detector does not reassemble the apostrophe splices quotePosixCommandArg emits in single-quoted arguments, so a quoted credential assignment inside a rendered argument with a line break is missed; the gap exists since pm-oq893h for arguments holding ! and widened when pm-jzrtsr single-quoted line breaks."
  "2026-11-26T17:32:24.253Z","harness:claude-code","Greptile on cf60e0226 (P2, confirmed against main, fixed): a Python prefixed string holding an escaped apostrophe before a dict member with a bare reference on the same line became a finding, because the shell reading closed the bytes string at the escaped apostrophe and declined its real closing quote. When a single-quoted string closes at an apostrophe a backslash precedes and only word characters follow it up to the next apostrophe, that apostrophe is the string's closing quote under Python's reading and declines nothing; a shell segment opened after a blank (the round-3 fixture) is still declined. Only single-quoted closes lend that reading. Spec cases added; the corpus differential is unchanged."
  "2026-11-26T17:34:11.858Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 5 on PR #1789 (Git ceilings, release retries and POSIX quoting reported clean): (1) fixed: a Python-prefixed string inside a container now judges its escapes by the context before the prefix, so a raw string holding an escaped apostrophe in a dict no longer shifts the later member's boundary. (2) deferred to pm-ghgf4a, both false positives with no lost finding: distrust after a declined quote is line-wide, so a prose apostrophe in a YAML flow scalar or a joined shell segment disables later boundaries of that kind; and the shell-word reading joins Python adjacent literals without decoding a hex escape between them."
  "2026-11-26T18:09:42.925Z","harness:claude-code","Greptile on 91d4ac17c and be1fd40bf (two P1 findings, confirmed against main, which reports both lines): the Python escaped-apostrophe exception and the prefix-context escapes each let a shell line hide a literal password. Both exceptions are reverted; a prefixed string escapes as an unprefixed one does, and the Python escaped-apostrophe false positive moves to pm-ghgf4a. Both shell lines are spec cases, and the corpus differential is unchanged with no lost literal finding."
  "2026-11-26T18:34:21.492Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit on 72f024f7b (Major, confirmed; main reports the line): a declined apostrophe left a later double-quoted string trusted, although in shell that double quote sits inside the single-quoted segment, so a cross-kind desync cut a literal. A declined quote of either kind now makes every later string on its line untrusted (one lexer flag). The cost is the prose-apostrophe-outside-strings false-positive class pm-ghgf4a already owns; both cross-kind lines are spec cases and the corpus differential is unchanged."
  "2026-11-26T18:45:35.764Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 6 on PR #1789 (narrow, changes since be1fd40bf): (1) fixed: the escape setting of the last string carried past its close, so after a raw string read without escapes an escaped double quote outside every string opened a phantom string and a bare reference became a finding; the lexer now resets escapes when a string closes, as outside every string a backslash escapes. (2) fixed: the lexer comment and MUTATION_INTEGRITY.md now say when a Python apostrophe is declined. (3) deferred to pm-ghgf4a: on a line whose cut is withheld, the accepted match can consume the next YAML member, so the finding is reported at the earlier placeholder rather than the later literal; a finding still fires."
  "2026-11-26T18:56:47.469Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 7 on PR #1789: a backslash before a quote outside every string is now a source of ambiguity (shell escape versus PowerShell path separator); a PowerShell line in which that desync let a phantom string cut a literal, missed by main since GH-1775, is now reported, and the reference-only lines this makes conservative are owned by pm-ghgf4a. The comment now names the ANSI-C exception; the ceiling and root-walk test issues are fixed on pm-gh1787's spec."
